# Vercel Speed Insights Installation

Successfully installed and configured Vercel Speed Insights for the LapSim-AI project.

## Changes Made

### 1. Package Installation
- Installed `@vercel/speed-insights` version 2.0.0 using pnpm
- Updated `package.json` to include the new dependency
- Updated `pnpm-lock.yaml` with the new package and its dependencies

### 2. Code Integration
Modified `web/src/app.ts`:
- Added import statement: `import {injectSpeedInsights} from '@vercel/speed-insights';`
- Called `injectSpeedInsights()` function immediately after the existing `inject()` call for analytics
- This follows the same pattern as the existing Vercel Analytics integration

## Implementation Details

This project uses Vite with vanilla TypeScript (not React). According to the official Vercel Speed Insights documentation, for non-framework projects, the `injectSpeedInsights()` function should be used to add the tracking script to the app.

The function was added in `web/src/app.ts` at line 12, immediately after the analytics injection, ensuring Speed Insights is initialized when the application starts.

## Verification

All verification steps completed successfully:
- ✅ TypeScript type checking passed (`pnpm typecheck`)
- ✅ Build completed successfully (`pnpm build`)
- ✅ All 36 tests passed (`pnpm test`)
- ✅ No linting errors or warnings introduced

## Framework Detection

This is a Vite-based project using vanilla TypeScript, not a React, Vue, or other framework-specific setup. The implementation correctly uses the vanilla JavaScript/TypeScript approach with the `injectSpeedInsights()` function from `@vercel/speed-insights`.

## Notes

- Speed Insights will not track data in development mode (this is intentional behavior)
- Data will start flowing once the app is deployed to Vercel
- The integration follows the same pattern as the existing Vercel Analytics setup
- All existing code structure was preserved, only adding the necessary Speed Insights import and initialization
